"text":"Every array reserves a specific amount of memory to hold its contents.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"When you add elements to an array and that array begins to exceed its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"reserved capacity, the array allocates a larger region of memory and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"copies its elements into the new storage. The new storage is a multiple","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"of the old storage’s size. This exponential growth strategy means that","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"appending an element happens in constant time, averaging the performance","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"of many append operations. Append operations that trigger reallocation","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"have a performance cost, but they occur less and less often as the array","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"grows larger.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The following example creates an array of integers from an array literal,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"then appends the elements of another collection. Before appending, the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"array allocates new storage that is large enough store the resulting","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"elements.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["var numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]","\/\/ numbers.count == 5","\/\/ numbers.capacity == 5","","numbers.append(contentsOf: stride(from: 60, through: 100, by: 10))","\/\/ numbers.count == 10","\/\/ numbers.capacity == 10"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"}]}],"kind":"symbol","hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/ArraySlice"]]},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/ArraySlice\/capacity